h(2, id: "source-layout") { 3. Source layout }

p {
    A document is a sequence of top-level blocks. The first token of each block
    is an identifier: the block type. After the last block the file ends.
    Anything else at top level is a parse error.
}

p {
    Allowed top-level names:
}

list {
    item { code{meta} }
    item { code{h} }
    item { code{p} }
    item { code{codeblock} }
    item { code{hr} }
    item { code{list} }
    item { code{quote} }
    item { code{image} }
    item { code{table} }
    item { code{callout} }
    item { code{raw} }
    item { code{math} }
    item { code{toc} }
    item { code{footnote} }
    item { code{include} }
    item { code{figure} }
    item { code{deflist} }
    item { code{details} }
}

h(3, id: "comments") { 3.1 Comments }

p {
    Line comments start with code{//} and run to the end of the line. Block
    comments are code{/* ... */} and may span lines. An unclosed block comment
    is a lex error. Comments are skipped in the token stream. They are not
    skipped inside raw scopes, inline code, or inline math.
}

codeblock(lang: "text", file: "comments.du") {!
// a whole line

p { visible /* hidden */ text }
!}

h(3, id: "strings-and-numbers") { 3.2 Strings and numbers }

p {
    String literals use double quotes. An unclosed string is a lex error.
    Escapes inside strings:
}

table {
    row(header: true) {
        cell { Sequence }
        cell { Result }
    }
    row {
        cell { backslash quote }
        cell { a quote }
    }
    row {
        cell { two backslashes }
        cell { a backslash }
    }
    row {
        cell { backslash n }
        cell { newline }
    }
    row {
        cell { backslash t }
        cell { tab }
    }
    row {
        cell { backslash r }
        cell { CR }
    }
}

p {
    Any other character after a backslash is kept as that backslash plus the
    character.
}

p {
    Bare integers are tokens. Heading level is the only place the grammar
    requires a number.
}

h(3, id: "identifiers") { 3.3 Identifiers }

p {
    An identifier starts with an ASCII letter or underscore. Later characters
    may be letters, digits, or underscore.
}

p {
    In two raw contexts the scanner also accepts hyphen: footnote ids written
    without quotes, and attribute names inside link parentheses. Quoted footnote
    ids may contain any string bytes.
}

h(3, id: "whitespace") { 3.4 Whitespace }

p {
    Inside paragraph-like bodies, runs of space, tab, CR, and LF collapse to a
    single space. Leading and trailing spaces on a body are trimmed. Line breaks
    in source do not become line breaks in HTML unless you use a codeblock, a
    raw block, or explicit HTML.
}

h(3, id: "raw-scopes") { 3.5 Raw scopes }

p {
    code{codeblock}, code{raw}, and code{math} take a body that starts with
    brace-bang and ends at the first unescaped bang-brace from that point. The
    body is not tokenized as DocUP.
}

p {
    If the first line after the opener is only spaces, tabs, or CR, that line is
    dropped. Trailing spaces and newlines are dropped. Empty content after that
    trim is a semantic error.
}

p {
    To put a bang-brace in the body, escape it:
}

list {
    item { backslash, bang, brace — stored as bang-brace }
    item { bang, backslash, brace — stored as bang-brace }
    item { two backslashes, bang, brace — stored as backslash, bang, brace }
}

h(3, id: "attributes") { 3.6 Attribute lists }

p {
    Many blocks take a parenthesized list after the name:
}

codeblock(lang: "text") {!
name(key: "value", flag: true, "positional")
!}

p {
    A named field is an identifier, a colon, then a string or the identifiers
    code{true} / code{false}. A string code{"true"} is also accepted where the
    generator compares against the text code{true}.
}

p {
    A bare string is a positional argument. Entries may be separated by commas.
    An empty code{()} is valid where the list is optional.
}

p {
    Which keys are read depends on the block. Unknown keys do not fail the
    parse. Heading attributes after the level are name/string pairs only;
    booleans are not accepted there.
}

h(3, id: "prose-escapes") { 3.7 Prose escapes }

p {
    In paragraph-like text, a backslash before one of these characters emits
    that character and does not start syntax:
}

list {
    item { backslash brace-open — a literal open brace }
    item { backslash brace-close — a literal close brace }
    item { backslash bang — a literal bang }
    item { two backslashes — a literal backslash }
}

p {
    Inside inline code, backslash-brace is unescaped to a brace and does not
    change brace depth. Inside inline math, backslash-brace is kept so KaTeX
    still sees it.
}
